<div class="r-back-band"><a class="r-back" href="from-the-oven.html">From the Oven</a></div>
<header class="r-masthead">
    <span class="r-eyebrow">From the Oven &middot; No. IV &middot; Every Season</span>
    <h1 class="r-title">Bakery-Style <em>Chocolate Chip Cookies</em></h1>
    <p class="r-lede">Brown butter, two kinds of chocolate, and a dough that's better for the wait.</p>
    <div class="r-meta"><div><span class="l">Makes</span><span class="v">18 large cookies</span></div><div><span class="l">Oven</span><span class="v">375&deg;F</span></div><div><span class="l">Time</span><span class="v">12 minutes, after the chill</span></div></div><div class="r-print"><button class="r-print-btn" onclick="window.print()">Print this recipe</button></div>
  </header>

  <section class="r-plate">
    <figure>
      <img src="../images/chocolate-chip-cookies.jpg" alt="Three chocolate chip cookies on a floral-rimmed plate, seen from above">
      <figcaption>Three down, fifteen to go</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="r-body"><div class="r-body-inner"><div class="r-headnote">
    <p>The butter is the part I look forward to. It foams, then quiets, then turns the color of maple syrup and smells like toffee &mdash; the whole downstairs knows what's coming before the first cookie is anywhere near the oven. Then comes the part nobody enjoys: the dough goes into the refrigerator, and you walk away. Two hours if you must. A full day if you can stand it. The flavor deepens overnight in a way no shortcut has ever matched.</p>
    <p>The chew comes from bread flour. The melty pools come from chopping the chocolate instead of pouring it from a bag. The rest comes from patience, which this recipe asks for twice and repays both times.</p>
  </div></div></div>

  <div class="r-sec">
    <div class="r-sec-label">Ingredients</div>
    <div class="r-group"><p class="r-group-label">The brown butter</p><ul class="r-ing"><li>1 cup (226 g) unsalted butter</li></ul></div>
    <div class="r-group"><p class="r-group-label">The dry</p><ul class="r-ing"><li>2 cups bread flour</li><li>1 cup all-purpose flour</li><li>1 tsp baking soda</li><li>1 tsp baking powder</li><li>1 tsp cornstarch</li><li>1 tsp kosher salt</li></ul></div>
    <div class="r-group"><p class="r-group-label">The wet</p><ul class="r-ing"><li>1&frac14; cups dark brown sugar</li><li>&frac12; cup granulated sugar</li><li>1 large egg</li><li>1 egg yolk</li><li>2 tsp vanilla extract</li></ul></div>
    <div class="r-group"><p class="r-group-label">The chocolate</p><ul class="r-ing"><li>10 oz chopped dark or bittersweet chocolate</li><li>4 oz semisweet chocolate chips, or chopped semisweet chocolate</li><li>Flaky sea salt, for finishing (optional)</li></ul></div>
  </div>

  <div class="r-aside"><p>Chopped chocolate melts into pools; chips hold their shape. Using both gives you the best of each in the same bite.</p></div>

  <div class="r-sec">
    <div class="r-sec-label">Method</div>
    <ol class="r-steps"><li>Brown the butter: melt it in a light-colored pan over medium heat, swirling now and then, until the foam settles and the solids at the bottom turn amber and smell nutty. Pour it into a large bowl, scraping in every browned bit, and let it cool until just warm.</li><li>Whisk together the bread flour, all-purpose flour, baking soda, baking powder, cornstarch, and kosher salt.</li><li>Mix the warm brown butter with both sugars until smooth.</li><li>Beat in the egg, the yolk, and the vanilla.</li><li>Fold in the dry ingredients just until no streaks of flour remain.</li><li>Fold in the chocolate.</li><li>Cover and chill the dough at least 2 hours. Twenty-four is best.</li><li>Heat the oven to 375&deg;F. Scoop the dough in 3&ndash;4 tablespoon mounds onto lined baking sheets, leaving room between them.</li><li>Bake 11&ndash;13 minutes, until the edges are golden and the centers still look slightly underdone. Finish with flaky salt while warm, if you like, and let them cool on the pan a full 10 minutes &mdash; they set as they rest.</li></ol>
  </div>

  <section class="r-plate">
    <figure>
      <img src="../images/chocolate-chip-cookies-plate.jpg" alt="Chocolate chip cookies on a floral plate beside a linen napkin">
      <figcaption>Set out for the afternoon</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="r-aside"><p>The dough keeps in the refrigerator for three days &mdash; or scoop it and freeze the mounds, and bake one or two straight from frozen, a minute or two longer. A warm cookie is then never more than fifteen minutes away.</p></div>

  <section class="r-note"><p>"The centers should worry you a little when they come out of the oven. Ten minutes on the pan, and they'll have sorted themselves out. Most things do."</p></section>

